<h2>The Brooklyn-London-Monaco Striker — Why the Story Matters</h2>
<p>Balogun's path to this tournament is one of the more extraordinary in American soccer history. He was born in Brooklyn because his Nigerian mother — visiting family in the United States — was seven months pregnant and not permitted to board her return flight to London. She gave birth to Folarin in New York. Two months later, the family returned to England, where he was raised.</p>
<p>He grew up in London. He joined Arsenal at age eight after a scout spotted him. He came through one of the most competitive youth academies in world football. He was eligible to represent England, Nigeria, or the United States. In May 2023 he chose the USA — in part because of USMNT captain Pulisic's personal appeal — and committed to a country he had left as a two-month-old infant.</p>
<p>He then scored two goals in his first World Cup match on home soil, becoming the first American to do so since the 1930s, in front of nearly 19 million American viewers — the highest-rated USA men's match ever broadcast.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">🏆</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>The Patenaude parallel:</strong> Bert Patenaude scored three goals against Paraguay at the 1930 World Cup — including what is believed to be the first hat-trick in World Cup history. Ninety-six years later, Balogun scored twice against Paraguay in the same tournament, in the same shirt, at a World Cup on American soil. The historical echo is not something Balogun could have planned. It happened because a pregnant woman in 2001 was not allowed onto an airplane.</div>
</div>
